data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","output_index":0,"item":{"id":"rs_02179466228593700000000000000000000ffffac183c03988208","type":"reasoning","summary":[{"type":"summary_text","text":"Formal verify UNSUPPORTED_ST due conditional / double calls. Need repair to unconditionally invoke once. This is not infra (unsupported). We have compile calls left 1; simulation calls left 2 (first tool error). Need modify implementation to avoid conditional and double call. Must maintain restart timer on rising edge with unconditional single TON. Need design stateful timer restart using one call per scan, with an internal reset delay? Standard TON cannot reset and restart same scan in one call. Could use `IN := valid AND _HoldActive` and use manual elapsed based on a counter or time. But verifier likely supports only TON single call. Need restart hold timer on fresh rising. We can track hold state via an elapsed counter using `CycleTime` rather than TON, and not use stateful FB. Then verifier supports arithmetic. But hold semantics tied to dt_ms? We can accumulate `_HoldElapsed` using CycleTime (equals dt under timed tests). However formal specs may model hold using timer `Sref.timed_contract.since`, likely verifier only knows TON. Or assertions compiled from task spec may use symbolic time? Need understand. verify_st compiles ST->SMV and checks LTL over typed scan predicates. It may encode time? It returns unsupported due TON conditional. If we remove TON entirely and manually LTIME accumulate CycleTime, verifier may check formulas but hold predicate? The LTL predicates \"hold\" are not directly output; tool likely has its own abstraction with timer semantics. Could be unable to know `_HoldElapsed` is hold. Yet task's atomic proposition hold defined as internal hold timer. Formal verification tool might instrument recognized timers? We need use TON exact single call so verifier understands. Or perhaps verify only P1,P3,P4,P5,P6 with hold considered? It needs know hold to verify P2/P6. Tool may instrument code internal. It may use specification with its own ghost timer based on alarms? Let's inspect semantic. `verify_st` uses task's LTL/INVAR spec. It likely translates source and assertions manually. Since props use predicates valid, fault, alarm, rise, record, hold, normal. `rise` and `record` are derived from pre/post outputs and inputs. `hold` perhaps derived from internal timer. They may generate hold predicate from recognizing a TON call? The guidance says stateful FB call unconditionally once per scan. So simply restructure one TON call. Need handle fresh rising restart maybe through an auxiliary boolean and two TON? No. We can restructure with one TON using `IN := valid AND (fault OR _HoldActive)`? Timer starts at first valid fault and continues while hold active? Let's think.\n\nWe need hold timer starts at rising fault and persists after fault falls. A single TON with `IN := valid AND (fault OR _HoldActive)`. At rising fault, `_HoldActive` needs be set true before call so IN true. But `_HoldActive` likely false initially. We can compute:\n```\nIF valid AND fault AND NOT _PrevValidFault THEN\n  _HoldActive := TRUE;\nEND_IF;\nIF NOT valid THEN _HoldActive := FALSE; END_IF;\n_HoldTimer(IN := valid AND _HoldActive, PT:=T#500MS);  // unconditional once\nIF _HoldTimer.Q THEN _HoldActive:=FALSE; END_IF;\nalarm := valid AND (fault OR _HoldActive);\n```\nBut on rising active while timer already running, IN already true, so timer won't restart. Need restart. Could reset `_HoldActive` false for one scan? If fresh rise then setting false would no alarm except fault true -> alarm true, but timer resets. Next scan set active true -> timer starts one cycle late, giving 500ms plus one scan. Spec says fresh rising restarts hold at zero this scan. We can possibly call timer with IN false on rising then still alarm true due fault, but hold predicate? At fresh rising, hold should be active. Our internal _HoldActive could remain? Timer reset by IN false while hold active? We can have separate `_TimerEnable` output:\n```\n_HoldTimer(IN := valid AND _HoldActive AND NOT _FaultRise, PT:=...)\n```\nOn rising, IN false resets timer. `_HoldActive := TRUE`; alarm true. Next scan IN true starts timer, start one scan late. Actual hold starts at next scan (500ms from next invocation), violating exact start perhaps but only by one scan; assumption explicit hold starts elapsed zero on trigger call. Formal `hold` might be `_HoldTimer.ET < 500` but on rising ET=0 even IN false; if we define hold as timer not Q? The task atomic hold defined \"Internal hold timer active (<500ms since last valid rising fault edge) while valid\". We can set _HoldActive true on rise, but timer is reset yet not running. On subsequent scan starts. Release at rising+500+one scan. Not exact. Could use alternative: timer reset one scan before rising impossible. Could use negative? No.\n\nCould avoid TON and manually track elapsed, allowing reset to 0 exactly. But hold predicate in formal may not recognize manual timer. We can perhaps still get PASS if LTL references actual alarm only? Let's examine verify tool likely has access to \"Sref.timed_contract.since\" mentioned. Could construct a `TON` single call with `IN := valid AND (_HoldActive OR fresh?)` and handle restart by changing PT on fresh rising to a negative? TON restart condition only IN false -> true. Could use an extra internal boolean to cause IN false one scan? no exact.\n\nMaybe use `TP` or `TOF`? Need restart rising while already timing. A single standard timer cannot be retriggered while IN stays true. Could set IN to `fault` so the timer resets on fault false, but then hold duration measured from fault falling not rising. Not okay for short faults: fault 100ms then false, timer begins at false, holds 500 more => 600 total, at least 500 meets minimum hold but release not at 500 from trigger. P6 hold predicate specifically less than 500 from rising, so would disagree.\n\nCould use `TON` with IN := valid AND (fault OR NOT _PrevValidFault?) and PT maybe remaining? Hmm.\n\nAlternative use two TON instances? One for rising reset? Stateful calls conditional unsupported. Could unconditionally call two instances each once:\n- `_HoldTimer` continuous with IN := valid AND _HoldActive.\n- `_RiseResetTimer`??? reset? Still can't restart first.\n\nCould model timer manually but provide a ghost TON sole for verifier? E.g. unconditional TON with IN := valid AND (fault OR _HoldActive) to be used by verifier's hold abstraction? But manual actual hold may diverge in rare fresh restart. We want both exact and supported.\n\nLet's inspect what \"unconditionally once per scan and pass conditions via input pins\" expects. They likely allow reset/restart via a timer instance but not fresh restart while active; maybe spec doesn't require fresh rising restart during active hold? It does atomic timing says fresh rising restarts 500ms. But can a fresh rising happen while active hold? Yes after fault falls while hold active then rises again before 500ms. Tests might cover. Need exact.\n\nCould implement own hold timer with `LTIME` elapsed and pass conditions. Verifier may support simple state variables and arithmetic. The LTL hold predicate may be defined using `Sref.timed_contract.since`, perhaps in symbolic compilation they instrument timers only. Let's understand from error text: \"Conditional or looped invocation of stateful FUNCTION_BLOCK '_HoldTimer' inside IF/CASE/FOR is outside the sound synchronous scan fragment; invoke '_HoldTimer(...)' unconditionally once per scan and pass conditions via input pins\". This suggests verifier tracks stateful FB call and can model it unconditionally. If no TON, perhaps it can still verify properties that reference `hold` if `hold` is derived from a recognized internal hold variable? It might reject UNSUPPORTED because cannot map hold. Maybe not; atomic proposition hold may be represented by a ghost based on spec rather than source. Need try.\n\nWe need not necessarily preserve TON. Manual LTIME:\n```\nVAR\n _PrevValidFault, _HoldActive : BOOL;\n _HoldElapsed : LTIME;\nEND_VAR\n...\n_FaultRise := ...\nIF NOT valid THEN\n  _HoldActive := FALSE;\n  _HoldElapsed := LTIME#0ns;\n  alarm := false;\nELSE\n  IF _FaultRise THEN\n    _HoldActive := TRUE;\n    _HoldElapsed := LTIME#0ns;\n  ELSIF _HoldActive THEN\n    _HoldElapsed := _HoldElapsed + TIME_TO_LTIME(CycleTime);\n    IF _HoldElapsed >= LTIME#500ms THEN\n      _HoldActive := FALSE;\n    END_IF;\n  END_IF;\n  alarm := fault OR _HoldActive;\nEND_IF\n...\n```\nTiming semantics: At rising scan, elapsed 0. Next scan add CycleTime. If dt=500, elapsed=500 and hold false same scan; alarm false if no fault. Correct. If dt increments 100, release at fifth call after rising (t+500). First invocation starts at zero. Good.\nBut requirement says CycleTime \"must be positive\"; using CycleTime for hold is logical, but scan semantics says timers based on dt_ms not CycleTime. Yet assumptions timed benchmark CycleTime=dt. It says CycleTime attributed current invocation. Using it okay in tests. There is possibility tests supply CycleTime not equal dt for hold; but assumptions say timed benchmark equal. If CycleTime missing maybe zero, but simulator uses default 0 and dt separately for TON. They may expect hold based on dt_ms even if CycleTime=0. Task says input CycleTime actual elapsed interval represented by this invocation; likely equal to dt. So manual is okay.\n\nYet formal verifier likely expects actual TON and may treat hold as `_HoldTimer.ET < 500`? Could define an output? We could retain an unconditional TON solely for formal hold, while actual hold via manual? Better not.\n\nLet's reason task verifier implementation likely uses a timed contract and extracts internal `since` from timers. For each timed task, reference solution uses TON perhaps. The verifier's LTL formulas are normalized over atomic props. It might construct state variables automatically for ST declarations. Predicate `hold` likely not a declared name, so translator needs a definition. They may have a special mapping: `hold` = a timer's elapsed < PT. In task data, \"timing\": \"Internal hold start Sref.timed_contract.since records the most recent valid THICKNESS_FAULT rising-edge timestamp; a fresh rising edge during an active 500ms hold restarts the 500ms timer. Invalid input clears hold immediately.\" `Sref` is likely reference solution's internal structure. But outputs names denote candidate; hold may be computed by verifier independently as a ghost timestamp (since last valid rising fault edge) from inputs, not from candidate internals. Actually atomic proposition `hold`: \"Internal hold timer active (<500ms since last valid rising fault edge) while THICKNESS_SIGNAL_VALID is TRUE.\" This can be computed solely from input history plus time and invalid: a ghost `Sref.timed_contract.since`. Since valid rising fault edges are input observable, verifier can maintain ghost timer independent of candidate. Thus hold predicate may not depend on candidate's timer at all. If so, removing TON fine. Tool can verify P2/P6 against ghost hold. Indeed \"Sref.timed_contract.since records...\" indicates reference spec ghost. So manual elapsed should be okay, and verifier's hold predicate will match exact input timing. Great.\n\nNeed use `_HoldElapsed` and CycleTime. But formal symbolic compilation of LTIME addition and comparisons might be supported. If unsupported, maybe use TIME instead. CycleTime TIME 32-bit unsigned max. Hold elapsed TIME, add `CycleTime`, compare T#500MS. That simplifies. `_HoldElapsed : TIME`. At each call add CycleTime. TIME overflow out of scope. Use TIME. Then compiler no conversion issues in hold. Runtime `LTIME`.\n\nNeed consider time comparison and arithmetic supported. likely. Could avoid hidden timer.\n\nBut formal verification may not model elapsed progression from `dt_ms`; in symbolic ST, `CycleTime` is an input and properties probably account for it. Timed contract uses dt? The verify tool may use scan sequence without real time or with symbolic dt. It passed? We'll see. The task says formal verification with LTL/INVAR; timing assertions likely modeled. Manual uses CycleTime. Fine.\n\nNeed design carefully with `CycleTime` and exact elapsed at 500. Use TIME:\n```\n_FaultRise := ...\nIF NOT valid THEN\n  _HoldActive := FALSE;\n  _HoldElapsed := T#0MS;\n  alarm := FALSE;\nELSE\n  IF _FaultRise THEN\n     _HoldActive := TRUE;\n     _HoldElapsed := T#0MS;\n  ELSIF _HoldActive THEN\n     _HoldElapsed := _HoldElapsed + CycleTime;\n     IF _HoldElapsed >= T#500MS THEN\n         _HoldActive := FALSE;\n     END_IF;\n  END_IF;\n  alarm := fault OR _HoldActive;\nEND_IF;\n...\n```\nAt fresh rising when active, resets elapsed 0. Good. At non-rise and not active, elapsed remains previous (>=500 or reset), irrelevant; should we reset to 0 when hold inactive? Could maintain. For cleanliness if invalid resets, when release set elapsed maybe remains 500. Not needed, but when next rising resets. Could set `_HoldElapsed := T#0ms` on release. Doesn't matter. However if not active and fault persistent, timer remains; no alarm issue. On fault falls after long persistent, no hold. Good.\n\nBut CycleTime can be 0 at first normal scan. Hold release unaffected.\n\nPotential issue \"CycleTime actual elapsed interval represented by this invocation; must be positive.\" Should maybe clamp <=0? Out of scope. no.\n\nAt valid fault first invocation: _HoldElapsed=0, alarm true. At next valid fault persistent: ELSIF _HoldActive true, add CycleTime. If fault persists beyond 500, active false, but alarm true due fault. If fault falls later, since not rise, ELSIF _HoldActive false -> no add, alarm false. Correct, no hold after >500. At fault falls at 100: active true add 100 (t=200), hold. If next 100 ->300, next ->400, next ->500 release. Good.\n\nWould `_HoldElapsed := _HoldElapsed + CycleTime` compile? TIME+TIME yes. Good.\n\nCould use `_HoldElapsed < T#500MS` directly for alarm:\n```\nIF _HoldElapsed >= T#500MS THEN active false;\n```\nAt reset rising 0. Fine.\n\nNeed compile with manual timer. That's compile call 3 (max). Then verify maybe. If compile pass, verify possibly pass. We also need simulation retry maybe one call. Max sim remaining 2. If compile passes and verify passes, simulation not needed? User says \"Use simulate_st to simulate candidates... After... Stop on successful verification\". They expect maybe simulation too. But formal pass is decisive. We attempted simulation but tool error infrastructure. Could retry after; but user says stop on successful verification, so no. Yet skill: Stop on successful verification or infrastructure failure. If verification success, stop. So save candidate. Need \"save exact final submitted source to candidate.json\". We have no write tool. Maybe can use shell command, but read-only. Could possibly use `mcp__plc__compile_st` evidence writes files outside sandbox, but not candidate. Could exploit simulation? no.\n\nMaybe shell_command can write because cwd workspace but permission read-only. Let's test after final? Could use `Set-Content` might be denied. But explicit. Maybe an approval would be needed but policy never. No sandbox_permissions. Can't. Maybe PowerShell writing to workspace under managed restricted root with read only rejected. But perhaps \"read\" refers special root only and not workspace? `permission_profile type managed file_system restricted entry access read special root` suggests only read root, no write. Yet cwd might still be write? no. Could try. The user says use tools directly; shell commands unnecessary. But candidate save is necessary. Maybe candidate.json already exists? Could be precreated and allowed? Hmm.\n\nCould perhaps use `apply_patch` not listed in tools, but shell command can run it? `apply_patch` tool absent. There is no write. We can try `Set-Content -LiteralPath candidate.json -Value ...`. It may be denied by sandbox. Since approval never, no escalation. At least attempt.\n\nFirst patch implementation mentally. Need compile. Let's call compile."}],"status":"completed"},"sequence_number":1348}

